WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.177 --> 00:00:01.560
(audience clapping)

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.393
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:02.393 --> 00:00:03.226
Have y'all ever met?

00:00:03.226 --> 00:00:04.059
Cara, have y'all ever met?

00:00:04.059 --> 00:00:04.892
- We have not.
- No.

00:00:04.892 --> 00:00:05.725
- You have not.

00:00:05.725 --> 00:00:06.633
Okay.
- It's my loss, obviously.

00:00:06.633 --> 00:00:07.466
- In my dreams.
I know.

00:00:07.466 --> 00:00:08.400
I don't stare too long.
- No, in my dreams.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.950
- You start forgetting anything,
you just look at her eyes.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:11.790
It's crazy.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.410
It's a magical power.
- Yeah, they're quiet lovely.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.924
- And then she'll guess what you're using.

00:00:14.924 --> 00:00:16.980
You, you've got like a
blue hue around yours.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.090
- Do I?
- [Kelly] Navy blue?

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.650
- Maybe it's the orange
that's reflecting off.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:20.483
It could be that.

00:00:20.483 --> 00:00:21.337
Could be that.

00:00:21.337 --> 00:00:22.170
- [Kelly] Is that the accent as well.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.370
- You should be a model.
- [Kelly] Yeah, right?

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:24.203
- She should be a model.

00:00:24.203 --> 00:00:25.036
You can get into that maybe,

00:00:25.036 --> 00:00:26.550
and win awards for it.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.920
So are you amazed with how much,

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.750
I mean people still love 'The Notebook.'

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.550
It's just one of the most
gigantic love stories of all.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.170
Did you know, did you have an inkling

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.770
that this is gonna connect
when you were writing it?

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.960
It's hard to say, I guess now, but.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.130
- You know, when I was writing it,

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.600
I thought it had a chance.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.433
It was funny.

00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:44.520
You know, it was a story

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.150
originally inspired by my
wife at the time, Kathy,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:48.993
her grandparents.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:48.993 --> 00:00:51.630
- And you know, I remember seeing how much

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.580
they cared for each other
toward the end of their life.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.410
And it was a story that moved me.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.303
And as I'm there typing,

00:00:59.965 --> 00:01:01.290
I hoped people would get it.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.323
But one never knows, right?

00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:04.470
- You don't, yeah.
- I had no agent.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:05.631
I knew nothing about
publishing at the time.

00:01:05.631 --> 00:01:06.570
- Wow.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:07.403
Geez.
- What?

00:01:07.403 --> 00:01:08.236
- Yeah.
- I didn't know that, man.

00:01:08.236 --> 00:01:09.690
I was working full-time.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.270
And then we had a young baby at the times

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.320
who didn't sleep well.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.000
So I had time in the evening to write

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.430
since Kathy would go to sleep.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.460
So I wrote 'The Notebook' in my spare time.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.171
- In your spare time.
- Yeah.

00:01:22.171 --> 00:01:23.820
(audience laughing)
- Just wrote 'The Notebook.'

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:24.930
It's fine.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.810
So have you ever wanted to
do anything besides writing?

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.300
Is there any other like
vocation or hobby that you love?

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.290
- Yeah.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.420
Well, sure you have what you think about

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:34.410
when you're a kid, right?

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:35.760
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- You'll be an astronaut.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.440
I thought that would be pretty
neat to be an astronaut.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.273
- Hell yeah, that's cool.

00:01:38.273 --> 00:01:39.106
- Right?
- Yeah.

00:01:39.106 --> 00:01:40.290
And there was a time I wanted to be

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.230
like an Olympic gold
medalist or a famous athlete.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.249
I thought that would be-

00:01:44.249 --> 00:01:45.082
- Dreaming small.

00:01:45.082 --> 00:01:45.935
- A pretty good.
- You dream small.

00:01:45.935 --> 00:01:47.880
- [Kelly] I'm like, you dream big.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:48.713
- Yeah.

00:01:48.713 --> 00:01:51.720
But I didn't have the talent
on the one hand, and you know,

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.000
the whole science thing on
the other didn't work out.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.740
So, it's hard to tell.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:56.670
You know, I started writing,

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.133
I think I wrote my first novel at 19.

00:01:59.133 --> 00:01:59.966
- [Cara] Wow.
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:01:59.966 --> 00:02:01.080
- And then it was never published.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.610
Wrote a second one at 22.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.680
It was never published.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.060
- See that's good for
writers to hear, I think.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.560
'Cause I think people think everything

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.840
is an overnight success, and it's not.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.160
You do have to grind a bit.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.330
Even if people don't see that

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:13.650
and they only see you when you start.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.660
- I would agree.
- Yeah.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.930
You have to learn your craft, right?

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:17.850
- Yeah.
- And so did that.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.190
And then when I was 28
I wrote 'The Notebook.'

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.380
But the neat thing about those first two

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.270
that were never published was that

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.750
when I went into writing 'The
Notebook' I knew I would finish.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:28.630
- [Cara] Yeah.

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:29.760
- You know, that was
never a question that I...

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.980
The only question I had
was whether I could make

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:32.813
the story good.

00:02:32.813 --> 00:02:34.140
And I had all the time in the world.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.670
- Well, well done.
- And I had no deadline.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:36.503
Thanks.

00:02:36.503 --> 00:02:37.336
Right.

00:02:37.336 --> 00:02:38.169
- People liked it.
(audience cheering)

00:02:38.169 --> 00:02:39.002
- Yeah.

00:02:39.002 --> 00:02:40.170
- People like many of them.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.690
But your latest book is
set in the world of music,

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:43.950
which we love.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:44.883
- Yeah.
- Yes.

00:02:44.883 --> 00:02:46.620
- So why did you choose that?

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.720
- Oh, there a few reasons.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.720
One was, I was always
interested in the idea

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.240
that falling in love is a little bit like

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.840
creating the perfect song,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.270
in that the notes have to be just right.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.430
The melody just has to be just right.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:09.840
The movement has to go.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.270
And if everything works out

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.820
you're left with this
beautiful song on the one hand.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.400
Or you're left with a really
beautiful relationship.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.330
And you know, wrong notes
can ruin the whole thing.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:22.163
- Yeah.

00:03:22.163 --> 00:03:23.760
- And so when you have
something that works in love

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.590
and then something that works in music,

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:26.970
I think they're very parallel.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.100
And so I kind of wanted to explore that

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.780
in part of the story.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.010
- Yeah, because you've written so many

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.660
so that's why I was wondering
why you went with this theme.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.190
- It's also different, right?
- Yeah.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:36.720
- It's something that
I haven't done before.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:37.553
- Yeah.

00:03:37.553 --> 00:03:40.900
- But in this, I wanted the
two characters to fall in love

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.840
while in the process of creating music.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.890
They literally write a song
together called, "Dreamland."

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:50.723
- Because I think sometimes

00:03:50.723 --> 00:03:52.010
when you write a song with
someone, it's like making a baby.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.434
- [Kelly] So vulnerable.

00:03:53.434 --> 00:03:54.267
- Yeah.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.290
- Again, we've gotta
stop talking about that.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.123
I know.

00:03:56.123 --> 00:03:57.663
I think someone wants a baby like.

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:01.140
'Cause she's talks about
making some babies over here.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.070
No, but it is you.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:03.330
It's such a vulnerable place.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.160
Funny enough, I'm not
good at sitting in a room

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:05.993
and writing with people.

00:04:05.993 --> 00:04:06.826
I'm not good at that.

00:04:06.826 --> 00:04:08.840
I'm good at like doing it on my own.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:09.810
- On own.
- Yeah.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.150
If you see my name on it,
I've really just done lyrics,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.230
usually and melody on my own.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.120
'Cause I'm not really open.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:15.953
It's a very-

00:04:15.953 --> 00:04:17.130
- Lyrics I write by myself always.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.800
But Melody, I need someone
to go stop adding things

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:20.824
'cause I'm constantly
like (indeterminable).

00:04:20.824 --> 00:04:21.870
- Yeah.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.633
But see that's a
relationship so you're right.

00:04:23.633 --> 00:04:24.600
Yeah, you're right.
- When you get in the room.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.470
- Well you know, so you
have these two people,

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.020
he generally writes music on his own

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.090
and then she struggles sometimes.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.450
She's good with some of the
lyrics but can't get it right.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:41.450
And it's a surprise to both
of them that it works so well.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:42.840
And of course they're-
- I love that.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.310
- Young and attractive

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.360
and they're at the beach-
- Yeah.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.620
- And there's walks.
- I mean.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.620
- They go kayaking and
then yeah, it all works.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.970
And then of course
there's a whole other part

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.020
of the story too.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.037
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- (gasps) Okay.

00:04:53.037 --> 00:04:53.870
- And it all comes together.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:54.703
- [Kelly] I love it.

00:04:54.703 --> 00:04:55.536
- That sounds amazing.

00:04:55.536 --> 00:04:56.760
- And also because been to a movie,

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:57.690
what about the soundtrack?

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:58.604
That's amazing.

00:04:58.604 --> 00:04:59.910
'Cause it's a musical
one and that's awesome.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:00.743
- Yeah.

00:05:00.743 --> 00:05:01.576
- It would be...

00:05:01.576 --> 00:05:02.730
Obviously that's the plan.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:03.563
- Yeah.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.500
- It's already actually
been sold film Universal,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.627
which is different from-

00:05:08.627 --> 00:05:11.190
- Yeah, I imagine when
your pen hits paper.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:12.111
- [Cara] Yeah.

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:13.620
- Then someone before
even reading the story

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.170
is like thank God, because
you're so talented at it

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:17.340
that people get excited about it.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.410
It's an opportunity, and you
have such a huge fan base,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.360
that people are already
excited when they know

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.260
you have a book coming out

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.850
and then they wanna see the movie.

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.380
There's already like a built-in fan base.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:26.238
That's incredible.

00:05:26.238 --> 00:05:27.071
- It is.
- [Cara] Yeah.

00:05:27.071 --> 00:05:28.200
- Do you find any pressure in that?

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.670
- Yeah?

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:30.930
- Oh that's a great question.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:31.980
Yes and no.
- Thank you.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:32.947
I just gotten here.
- Right, right.

00:05:32.947 --> 00:05:34.830
(audience laughs)

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.050
It's interesting when I sit down,

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.240
before you write first you
have to think of the story,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.073
right?
- Yeah.

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:43.830
- And so before I decide what to write

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.590
I try to make sure that
the idea will be original

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:50.190
not only for a novel,
but also for a movie,

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.190
because I know that it might...

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.050
So it's gotta meet both of those.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.890
So I throw stories away that I
say well it would be original

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:02.223
as a book but not as a film.

00:06:04.199 --> 00:06:05.032
And that's happened.

00:06:05.032 --> 00:06:06.540
And the best way to understand that,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:07.800
and it's not what I've ever done,

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.850
but let's say I would never
write a novel with a love story

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.723
based on the Titanic.

00:06:13.723 --> 00:06:14.556
- Right?
- Yeah.

00:06:14.556 --> 00:06:15.389
- Right?

00:06:15.389 --> 00:06:16.222
- It probably didn't end well.

00:06:16.222 --> 00:06:18.030
- Well it probably didn't, you know.

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:19.063
But certainly there's
not a book like that.

00:06:19.063 --> 00:06:19.896
- They didn't make it.

00:06:19.896 --> 00:06:21.600
- But everyone remembers the film.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.900
So if I did anything similar,
it just wouldn't be original.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:25.733
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:25.733 --> 00:06:27.180
- So lots of ideas get thrown away.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:28.920
- Do you have to kind
of relinquish control

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.470
when it comes to becoming
a movie a little bit?

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.870
Not like let go of kind of the outcome

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.790
of what you may imagine in your head.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:36.887
- You entrust it to someone, yeah.

00:06:36.887 --> 00:06:38.632
- That's a very sweet way of saying it.

00:06:38.632 --> 00:06:39.714
Of course you relinquish (laughs).

00:06:39.714 --> 00:06:40.965
- (laughs) Yeah.
- And they bought it.

00:06:40.965 --> 00:06:42.168
- You have to, but you're
like, I don't, I wanna...

00:06:42.168 --> 00:06:43.517
- You literary sign a contract-

00:06:43.517 --> 00:06:44.634
- But at this point though

00:06:44.634 --> 00:06:45.467
- That say they could do anything.

00:06:45.467 --> 00:06:46.300
- I know.
- You need to probably pick

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.177
and choose, so that's kind of awesome.

00:06:47.177 --> 00:06:48.010
- Right.

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:48.843
It is.

00:06:48.843 --> 00:06:50.730
And usually I work with
people, in fact always,

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.782
I worked with people who
liked the story in the novel.

00:06:53.782 --> 00:06:54.615
- So that's right, yeah.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.100
- And my goal then at
that point is to just have

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.330
the best film that you can

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:01.750
'cause the novels are already done.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:02.583
- [Cara] Yeah.

00:07:02.583 --> 00:07:05.190
- And so even if that has
some changes from the book-

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:06.023
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:06.023 --> 00:07:07.050
- To make the film better,

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.370
I'm generally okay with it.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.660
- Interestingly enough, I
don't know if you do this

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:13.530
but when I write music, I
see a movie playing out.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:14.520
There's so much imagery.
- Oh it's so visual.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.070
Yeah.
- I can't not do it.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.350
Imagery always naturally
happens whenever I'm...

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.269
It's like mini videos in my head.

00:07:21.269 --> 00:07:22.102
- Great.
- Interesting.

00:07:22.102 --> 00:07:22.935
- Yeah.

00:07:22.935 --> 00:07:24.000
Well Nicholas Sparks latest novel

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.500
is called "Dreamland", everybody.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.243
And it is out now.

